// src/lsp/lsp_hover.zen
// The one query, and the reason this stage was worth starting.
//
// A hover is a position, the thing under it, and what the checker
// already concluded about that thing:
//
//     position -> to_pos -> told_at -> a memo -> Types.name_of
//
// and `tests/corpus/sema_zen/a_cursor_position_finds_its_node` is the
// same round trip WITHOUT a server, which is what makes it the thing to
// read before this file. Nothing here is a query; everything here is a
// call into `src/ast/` and `src/sema/`, which is `DESIGN.md:45`'s "a
// thin server over compiler internals" meant literally.
//
// `DESIGN.md` decision 3 is why the memos are READ and not recomputed:
// "ask `type_of` twice and the second answer costs a `Map` lookup,
// which is the property an LSP needs, and the reason hover is not a
// recompile." `check_all` has already typed every node in the document,
// so this file never builds a `Ctx` and never calls `type_of`.
//
// FOUR ANSWERS, AND NOT ONE. An earlier version of this file read
// `expr_memo` and nothing else, and the consequence was measurable:
// probing every identifier in
//
//     add = (a: i32, b: i32) i32 { s = a + b; s }
//
// exactly three positions answered — `a`, `b` and `s` where they are
// USED — and the nine that did not were the function's own name, every
// parameter at its declaration, the local at its declaration, and every
// `i32`. A user hovers a declaration to ask what something IS at least
// as often as a use, and hovering a type name is how a type gets found
// out about. `lsp_decl.zen` is the finder that made the other three
// reachable; this file is what each one is said as:
//
//   Sig      a function's name  -> its declaration, reprinted from the
//                                  AST's names and the checker's types
//   OfType   a written type     -> what `type_from_ast` resolved it to
//   OfValue  an expression      -> what `type_of` gave it
//   Nothing  everything else    -> `null`, and see below
//
// A WRONG ANSWER IS WORSE THAN NO ANSWER, and that is the rule every
// arm below is written against. `null` reads to a user as "hover is not
// supported here"; a type reads as truth. So every path here answers
// from a memo the checker WROTE, and a miss anywhere in a compound
// answer — one parameter whose type never resolved, a signature with no
// return type — discards the whole answer rather than shipping a
// partial one. What that costs is listed at the bottom of this file.
//
// TWO WAYS IN, AND THE WORKSPACE DECIDES. `hover_in` checks the document
// as part of a BUILD and is what an editor reaches; `hover_at` checks it
// AS ONE MODULE, standing alone, and is what is left when there is no
// root to build — a client that sent no `rootUri`, or the two-file form
// of `zen lsp` whose document is on no disk. In the second, an import
// resolves to nothing and a name from another file has no type, which
// hover says by answering `null` rather than by guessing.
//
// AND IT REBUILDS PER HOVER. There is no cache across requests, because
// `design_lsp.md` §5 already established there is no unit smaller than
// the program and `PLAN.md:317` already named where the fix goes if
// that is too slow — stage 0.3, not here. A server that cached a
// `Checker` keyed on nothing would be a correctness bug bought with
// speed nobody measured.

str, String = std.text
Map = std.collections
Alloc, AllocError = std.mem
scan, Source = lex
Parser = parse
Ast, ExprId, TypeId, Span, Function = ast
Checker = sema.sema_check
check_all = sema.sema_decl
// THE DRIVER, REACHED FROM THE SERVER, and `design_lsp.md` §5 is where
// that inversion was argued and accepted: the alternative is a private
// copy of `walk` in this folder, which is the second implementation
// that document exists to prevent.
Build = zen.zen_build
root_for, relative_to = zen.zen_path
// `Types` IS IMPORTED FOR ITS METHODS, not to be named below.
// `c.types.write_name` is a method on `Types`, and without this line the
// bootstrapper resolves the name against `sema_diag` instead and reports
// that `write_name` is not exported there — a resolution failure that
// reads as a missing function in a module this file never mentions.
Types, TyId = sema.sema_ty
to_pos, to_wire, WirePos = lsp.lsp_pos
Tell, told_at = lsp.lsp_decl

// What a hover found. `found` is false for a position covered by
// nothing AND for one whose type the checker never wrote down — see
// above on why those two are one answer here.
//
// `shown` is TEXT AND NOT A TYPE NAME. It was called `type_name` while
// the only answer was a type; a function's name answers with its
// signature, and a field promising a type name while carrying a
// declaration is the kind of small lie that gets read as a bug later.
Hover* = {
    found*: bool,
    shown*: str,
    start*: WirePos,
    end*: WirePos,
}

// The type under a wire position in `text`, and the span to underline.
//
// `name` is what the document is called for the purpose of spans. It is
// the CLIENT'S URI, verbatim, and never a filesystem path: this file
// hands the same string to `Source` and to `told_at`, so the two agree
// by construction, and no `file://` conversion is needed or written.
// That conversion becomes necessary the moment a second file is
// involved — see this folder's root for what is not built.
hover_at* = (a: Alloc, name: str, text: str, line: usize, character: usize)
            Res<Hover, AllocError> {
    tree ::= a.Ast();
    lexed = scan(a, Source(file: name, text: text)).try();
    p ::= a.Parser(lexed, tree);
    m = p.module("doc").try();
    p.tree.add_module(m).try();
    c ::= a.Checker(p.tree).try();
    check_all(c).try();

    // THE CONVERSION HAPPENS EXACTLY HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE ON THE WAY
    // IN. Below this line every position is a Zen position.
    pos = to_pos(text, line, character);
    said(c, a, text, told_at(c.tree, name, pos))
}

// THE SAME QUESTION, ASKED OF A BUILD — which is the only way an
// IMPORTED name has ever had a type.
//
// `hover_at` above checks the document standing alone, so `Vec`, `str`
// and every name a real file opens with resolve to nothing, sema interns
// poison for them, and `write_ty_id` — correctly — answers `null`. That
// is hover working on a toy file and going quiet exactly where a person
// works.
//
// THREE THINGS AND NOT ONE OVERLAY. `Build.overlay` really is one field
// and one branch; what made this a change rather than an edit is that
// the driver had no public entry that kept anything (`Build.whole`) and
// nothing handed a `Checker` back (`Build.checked`). §5 has the revised
// price.
//
// THE OPEN DOCUMENT IS THE ENTRY, not `main`. A file being edited is
// usually not on the path from the program's entry — a library module, a
// file nothing imports yet — and walking from `main` would answer `null`
// for exactly those. Walking from the document reaches what the document
// IMPORTS, which is what its names mean, and `folder_root` in the walk
// is what pulls the sibling modules that complete a UFCS pool.
//
// THE COMPILATION ROOT IS COMPUTED AND NOT THE WORKSPACE. `root_for`
// says why: an editor opens at the repository and this tree builds as
// `zen build src`.
//
// `rel` AND NOT THE URI IS WHAT SPANS CARRY. A build names a module by
// its path relative to the root, so `told_at` is asked with the same
// string `Source` was given — the property `hover_at` got for free by
// using the URI for both.
hover_in* = (env: Env, a: Alloc, workspace: str, path: str, text: str,
             line: usize, character: usize) Res<Hover, AllocError> {
    root = root_for(env, a, workspace, path);
    docs ::= a.Map<str, str>();
    docs.set(path, text).try();
    b ::= a.Build(env, root);
    c = b.whole(relative_to(root, path), docs).try();
    pos = to_pos(text, line, character);
    said(c, a, text, told_at(c.tree, relative_to(root, path), pos))
}

// A position in nothing is not a failure — `ast_find.zen` says so and
// an editor hovering over a comment or over whitespace gets exactly
// this. The server turns it into a `null` result, which is what the
// protocol wants and is not an error either.
no_type* = () Hover {
    Hover(found: false, shown: "",
          start: WirePos(line: 0, character: 0),
          end: WirePos(line: 0, character: 0))
}

// The `Tell`, said. One `String` for the whole answer, filled by
// whichever arm applies, and thrown away unread when the arm could not
// finish it.
said = (c :: Checker, a: Alloc, text: str, t: Tell) Res<Hover, AllocError> {
    out ::= a.String().try();
    t.match({
        Nothing    => nothing_found(),
        Sig(f)     => wired(text, f.name.span, write_decl_sig(c, f, out).try(), out),
        OfType(ta) => wired(text, ta.span, write_ty(c, ta.id, out).try(), out),
        OfValue(v) => wired(text, v.span, write_val(c, v.id, out).try(), out),
    })
}

nothing_found = () Res<Hover, AllocError> { Ok(no_type()) }

// The span converted back out, and `known` carried through untouched.
// THE ONLY PLACE A HOVER LEARNS ITS WIRE COORDINATES, which is
// `lsp_pos.zen`'s rule obeyed from the outside.
wired = (text: str, span: Span, known: bool, out :: String)
        Res<Hover, AllocError> {
    Ok(Hover(found: known,
             shown: out.view(),
             start: to_wire(text, span.start),
             end: to_wire(text, span.end)));
}

// --- the three answers -------------------------------------------------

// A WRITTEN TYPE, resolved. `type_from_ast`'s memo is keyed on exactly
// this `TypeId`, so `i32` costs a `Map` lookup and nothing else.
//
// WHAT THIS ANSWERS FOR `i32` IS `i32`, and that weakness is the answer
// and not a placeholder for one. Three things make it worth having
// anyway, and they are the reason this was chosen over a description or
// a size:
//
//   - It is the RESOLVED type, not the written one. `Alias = Shape`
//     hovers as `Shape`; `Res<Cfg, _>` hovers with the `_` filled in as
//     the checker filled it; a `Vec<T>` inside a generic hovers with
//     the `T` it actually stands for. Where the source and the answer
//     agree, they agree because nothing was hidden.
//   - A NAME THAT RESOLVES TO NOTHING ANSWERS `null`. So a hover that
//     says `i32` is also saying "this name is a type the checker knows",
//     which is exactly the question a typo raises and the one thing a
//     tautology could not tell you.
//   - Anything richer — a struct's fields, a doc comment, a size — is
//     `documentSymbol`'s or `definition`'s answer wearing hover's
//     clothes, and both are listed in `design_lsp.md` §2 with the
//     queries they need. Inventing a fourth shape here would put the
//     description of a type in a file that must not describe types.
write_ty = (c :: Checker, id: TypeId, out :: String) Res<bool, AllocError> {
    c.type_memo.get(id).match({
        None     => no_answer(),
        Ok(memo) => write_ty_id(c, memo, out),
    })
}

// AN EXPRESSION, from the memo the checker filled as it typed the body.
//
// A MISS IS ANSWERED AS "NOTHING" and not as a made-up type.
// `design_lsp.md`'s open question 6 asks whether `expr_memo` holds an
// answer for every expression after `check_all` and records that it is
// unverified; until someone verifies it, inventing a name for an
// unreached node would put a wrong type in front of a user and call it
// a feature. `null` is the answer that cannot mislead.
write_val = (c :: Checker, e: ExprId, out :: String) Res<bool, AllocError> {
    c.expr_memo.get(e).match({
        None     => no_answer(),
        Ok(memo) => write_ty_id(c, memo, out),
    })
}

// THE ONE PLACE A `TyId` BECOMES TEXT, and the one place poison is
// stopped.
//
// `Ty.Unknown` is what sema interns for "a type I could not compute and
// have already reported", and `Types.write_name` spells it `<unknown>`.
// Printing that is the exact failure this stage was told not to commit:
// a user who hovers `AllocError` in a document whose import did not
// resolve gets a hover that looks like an answer and names a type that
// does not exist. `null` is what "I do not know" is spelled on this
// wire, so an unsettled type takes that route and the reason is the
// header's: a wrong answer is worse than no answer.
//
// AND IT WAS THE COMMON CASE, not a corner. Before `hover_in`, every
// imported name in every file resolved to poison — which this gate
// turned into silence rather than into a hover confidently naming a
// type that does not exist. It is what made the gap measurable: 4 of 12
// on a file that imports something, against 10 of 12 on one that does
// not. It still applies wherever there is no workspace to build.
write_ty_id = (c :: Checker, id: TyId, out :: String) Res<bool, AllocError> {
    poison_free(c, id).match({
        false => no_answer(),
        true  => { c.types.write_name(id, out).try(); an_answer() },
    })
}

// Does this type, anywhere inside it, contain poison?
//
// STRUCTURAL AND NOT TEXTUAL. Asking whether the rendered name contains
// the substring `<unknown>` would be shorter and would put a second
// spelling of sema's marker in this folder, where nothing else knows
// how a type is printed. `Ty` is a closed nine-variant enum, so the
// walk is total and the compiler says so when a variant is added.
//
// `Infer` is NOT poison and is deliberately let through: `_` in
// `Res<Cfg, _>` is a hole law 6 permits, sema reported nothing about
// it, and `_` is an honest thing to show a user who wrote one.
poison_free = (c :: Checker, id: TyId) bool {
    c.types.at(id).match({
        Unknown   => false,
        Res(r)    => poison_free(c, r.value) && poison_free(c, r.error),
        Named(n)  => all_poison_free(c, n.args),
        Fn(f)     => poison_free(c, f.ret) && all_poison_free(c, f.params),
        Union(u)  => all_poison_free(c, u.members),
        Array(ar) => poison_free(c, ar.elem),
        _         => true,
    })
}

all_poison_free = (c :: Checker, ids: Vec<TyId>) bool {
    every ::= true;
    ids.loop((h, id) { every = every && poison_free(c, id); });
    every;
}

// A FUNCTION'S NAME ANSWERS WITH ITS DECLARATION, reprinted:
//
//     add = (a: i32, b: i32) i32
//
// and not with `(i32, i32) i32`, which is what `Types.write_name` would
// produce from the function's type. The difference is the parameter
// names, and `ast_node.zen` already made the argument for keeping them:
// "`(i32, i32) i32` says nothing about which `i32` is which". They are
// not in the TYPE — sema is right that two signatures differing only in
// parameter names are one type — so they have to come from the AST, and
// that is the whole reason this is assembled here rather than interned
// as a `Ty.Fn` and printed.
//
// NOTHING IN IT IS INVENTED. Every name is an `Ident` the author wrote,
// every type is a `TyId` the checker resolved, and the `=` and `::` are
// the form the declaration was written in. Bounds on type parameters
// are the one thing dropped — `<T: Eq + Hash>` prints as `<T>` — because
// a bound is a `TypeId` list whose members are types in their own right
// and printing them would double the length of the common case to serve
// the rare one. Say so here rather than let a reader assume `T` is
// unbounded.
write_decl_sig = (c :: Checker, f: Function, out :: String) Res<bool, AllocError> {
    out.add_bytes(f.name.text).try();
    out.add_bytes(" = ").try();
    write_tparams(f, out).try();
    out.add_bytes("(").try();
    whole ::= true;
    f.params.loop((h, i, prm) {
        (i > 0).then(() { out.add_bytes(", ").try() });
        out.add_bytes(prm.name.text).try();
        out.add_bytes(binder(prm.mutable)).try();
        whole = write_opt_ty(c, prm.type, out).try() && whole;
    });
    out.add_bytes(") ").try();
    Ok(write_opt_ty(c, f.ret, out).try() && whole);
}

write_tparams = (f: Function, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    (f.tparams.len == 0).match({
        true  => Ok(()),
        false => write_tparam_list(f, out),
    })
}

write_tparam_list = (f: Function, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    out.add_bytes("<").try();
    f.tparams.loop((h, i, tparam) {
        (i > 0).then(() { out.add_bytes(", ").try() });
        out.add_bytes(tparam.name.text).try();
    });
    out.add_bytes(">")
}

// `self :: @Self` and `a: i32` are written differently and the
// difference is load-bearing — it is whether the parameter may be
// mutated — so a reprinted signature keeps it.
binder = (mutable: bool) str {
    mutable.match({ true => " :: ", false => ": " })
}

// A TYPE THAT WAS NOT WRITTEN IS NOT GUESSED. `Param.type` is absent in
// a closure and `Function.ret` in a lambda; a DECLARATION writes both,
// so an absent one here means the node is not the shape this answer
// assumed. Rather than print `()` and be wrong for a reader who cannot
// see which it was, the whole signature is discarded and the hover is
// `null`.
write_opt_ty = (c :: Checker, t: Res<TypeId>, out :: String)
               Res<bool, AllocError> {
    t.match({
        None   => no_answer(),
        Ok(id) => write_ty(c, id, out),
    })
}

// The two answers to "did that resolve", as functions so that sibling
// match arms have one type between them rather than a bare `Ok` beside
// a `Res<bool, AllocError>`.
an_answer = () Res<bool, AllocError> { Ok(true) }

no_answer = () Res<bool, AllocError> { Ok(false) }

// --- what is still `null`, on purpose ----------------------------------
//
// Listed rather than left to be discovered, because every one of them is
// a position where a user WILL hover and the reason it answers nothing
// is a fact about the compiler and not about this file:
//
//   - A STRUCT'S OR ENUM'S OWN NAME (`Foo` in `Foo = { .. }`).
//     `type_memo` is keyed on a written `TypeId` and a declaration site
//     has none. A USE of `Foo` hovers; its declaration does not.
//     Fixing it means building the type a declaration denotes, which is
//     `src/sema/sema_type.zen`'s `type_from_ast` and not reachable from
//     a name.
//   - A PATTERN BINDER (`n` in `Ok(n) => ..`). There is no pattern
//     memo, and `sema_check.zen`'s `Binding` is released at scope exit,
//     so nothing about it survives `check_all`. `design_lsp.md` §2 has
//     the same gap written down for go-to-definition on a local and
//     prices the fix at L3: a `span` on `Binding`, in `src/sema/`.
//   - A TYPE PARAMETER AT ITS DECLARATION (`T` in `<T>`). Same shape as
//     a struct name: the declaration is an `Ident`, and the `Ty.Var` it
//     denotes is interned against an owner this file cannot name. `T`
//     used as a type hovers, because a use is a written type node.
//   - AN IMPORTED NAME, WHEN THERE IS NO WORKSPACE. With one it answers,
//     because `hover_in` checks the document as part of a build. Without
//     one, `Vec` in `Vec = std.collections` still has no type, and the
//     import DECLARATION itself is `Nothing` either way — `decl_told`
//     answers no `Tell` for an `Import`, the same shape as a struct's
//     own name above.
//   - A CLOSURE'S PARAMETER. `Param.type` is absent there by
//     construction; the type comes from the position the closure is
//     passed at, and the parameter records nothing.
